# Stable view-row contract.

(mod view_api)

# A turn is two calls with the blocking read between them, and the split is the
# whole point of this contract. Both messages share one return convention:
# `nil` means "call me again", and a value means "this view is finished, and
# that value is its result".
#
# `show_prompt` renders whatever asks for the next line. `handle_line` is given
# that line — `nil` at EOF — and decides what it means. The caller owns the
# loop *and* owns the read: reading the user's own stdin is host authority, not
# the plugin's, so it happens between the two calls and under no budget at all.
# A view that read its own line spent its `^timeout_ms` waiting for a human and
# expired at the keyboard; the `chat` shell died on the prompt after a 150 s
# pause, with the whole budget consumed inside `$os/read_line`. Neither message
# can block on a person, so bounding both is honest.
(protocol HarnessView
  (message show_prompt [h] : Any)
  (message handle_line [h, line] : Any))

(fn view_provider [view : HarnessView] : HarnessView view)
